# CRITICAL — DOUBLE FREE from safe, ordinary syntax. BOTH backends.
#
# Reading an element out of a `Vector[Option[<heap type>]]` and unwrapping it
# double-frees the heap. `gg check` reports "OK: no semantic errors" (rc 0, no
# diagnostic of any kind), `gg build` exits 0 on C AND on `--backend=llvm`, and
# the program dies SIGABRT (rc 134).
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO ASSIGNMENT AND NO LOOP REQUIRED. This is NOT the `for x in coll:
# x = v` element-rebind double free (filed separately, and whose root cause is
# the for-loop element binder). This shape never writes anything -- it only
# READS an element and unwraps it -- so it is a DISTINCT mechanism that happens
# to share a symptom. The for-loop form below is included only to show that the
# loop is incidental, not causal.
#
# Measured at HEAD (2026-08-17), all four cells, C and LLVM:
#     Vector[Option[String]] via .get(0).unwrap() then .unwrap()  -> SIGABRT 134
#     Vector[Option[String]] via `for o in d: o.unwrap()`         -> SIGABRT 134
#     Vector[Option[int]]    (scalar payload), same shapes        -> correct
#     bare `Option[String]` with no container at all              -> correct
#
# The discriminator is a COLLECTION ELEMENT whose type is `Option[<heap type>]`.
# Container-free Options are fine and scalar payloads are fine.
#
# ⚠ THE PAYLOAD MUST BE HEAP-FORCED. `[Some("lit")]` runs CLEAN -- a string
# literal is static and never freed, so a literal-payload fixture is a FALSE
# NEGATIVE that would sit green forever. `mk()` exists solely to force a real
# heap allocation the optimizer cannot fold away.
#
# INTENDED: this program prints `aa` and exits 0. Crashing on a read is never
# acceptable, and there is nothing here to reject -- every construct is safe,
# documented, ordinary Gorget.
#
# Wired test: security_vector_option_heap_payload_double_free (tests/security.rs,
# #[ignore]d). It asserts the INTENDED output, so it fails today and goes green
# when the payload-ownership defect is fixed. Graduate it out of `known_gaps`
# semantics (drop the #[ignore]) that round.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[Option[String]] d = [Some(mk("a", "a"))]
    Option[String] o = d.get(0).unwrap()
    print(o.unwrap())
